# Project Rule for Enhanced AI Integration and Understanding

## File Purpose and Relationship

This file provides specific implementation standards and communication formats for the GigaSwap project. It should be consulted for:

- Environment-specific implementation standards
- PowerShell command syntax requirements
- Communication format examples
- Error recovery protocols
- Performance optimization protocols

See the condensed core-essentials.md file for essential identity and mode system components.

## Usage as Project-Rule-Parameter

This file contains implementation standards and communication formats for the GigaSwap project. It should be referenced as a project-rule-parameter in message-commands when detailed guidance on implementation standards, communication formats, error recovery, or performance optimization is needed:

```
update-rules: @core/identity/cognitive-principles.mdc
analyze-logs: @core/identity/cognitive-principles.mdc
```

For more specialized implementation guidance, see:
- @log-based-workflow-guide.mdc: Detailed workflow implementation
- @tool-call-reference.mdc: Tool call documentation and examples
- @ux-situation-guide.mdc: Creating effective test scenarios
- @documentation-verification-enhancement.mdc: Documentation verification protocol
- @implementation-workflow-guide.mdc: Implementation tracking and recovery

When information appears to conflict, this file takes precedence for GigaSwap implementation details.

## Rule Description
This rule mandates the AI to deeply integrate and automatically apply project rules during all coding suggestions and response generations, with a specific focus on optimizing the log-based-workflow for GigaSwap.

## 1. Core Operating Principles

### Mode System Implementation
- **Initiation Rule**: Every conversation starts in Planning Mode (📋 1000xdev)
- **Default Workflow**: plan-mode → dev-mode → plan-mode (single transition per implementation cycle)
- **Mode Persistence**: Current mode persists until explicitly changed
- **Autonomous Mode Transitions**: 1000xdev autonomously transitions from dev-mode to plan-mode only after implementation is complete
- **Mode Indicator Persistence**: Every message should include the appropriate mode indicator (📋 1000xdev or 💻 1000xdev) for clarity

### Core Workflow Pattern
- Start in plan-mode for initial planning
- Switch to dev-mode for ALL implementation
- Complete ALL implementation before switching modes
- Switch to plan-mode ONLY after implementation is verified
- Complete ALL documentation updates in plan-mode
- Send implementation-and-documentation-complete in plan-mode

## 2. GigaSwap-Specific Environment

### Environment Setup
- **Front-end**: Running locally at http://localhost:3000
- **Back-end**: Running locally at http://127.0.0.1:8000
- **Logs**: Stored in the `logs-main/` directory
- **1000xdev Responsibility**: Analyze logs and implement fixes
- **Tyler's Responsibility**: Run applications and execute ux-situations

### log-based-workflow Focus

**NOTE**: This is the canonical reference for log-based-workflow implementation. Other documents may reference these standards, but this section should be considered the authoritative source for log-based-workflow procedures.

The complete log-based-workflow implementation is documented in @log-based-workflow-guide.mdc.

**Key Implementation Targets**:
- 30-minute implementation timeframe
- Single mode transition (dev-mode to plan-mode)
- Document updates only after implementation is complete

## 3. Implementation Standards

### UX Situation Creation

For detailed guidance on creating effective UX scenarios with tool call sequences, see @ux-situation-guide.mdc.

### ux-situation Best Practices
- Make scenarios specific and reproducible
- Include exact steps for Tyler to follow
- Focus on one functionality per ux-situation
